WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:01.390
There's a pencil, right?

00:00:01.390 --> 00:00:02.640
That's all I need is a pencil.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.410
And a piece of paper
would come in handy.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.270
And I'm about to
write some dialogue,

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:09.194
and it's going to take place
in one place with two chairs,

00:00:09.194 --> 00:00:10.110
and that's all I need.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:14.970
How do you teach
people about drama?

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.397
You cannot learn how to write
drama without writing plays,

00:00:18.397 --> 00:00:19.980
putting it on in
front of an audience,

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.010
and getting humiliated.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.640
[DRAMATIC MUSIC]

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.057
Hemingway said,
"Writing is easy--

00:00:25.057 --> 00:00:27.223
all you have got to do is
sit down at the typewriter

00:00:27.223 --> 00:00:28.020
and bleed."

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:30.481
Throw away all the distractions
and write something.

00:00:30.481 --> 00:00:32.439
Every play needs to have
a beginning, a middle,

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:32.939
and an end.

00:00:32.939 --> 00:00:35.670
Jean-Luc Godard said, not
necessarily in that order,

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:38.074
and that's why French
movies are so F-ing boring.

00:00:38.074 --> 00:00:38.740
[DRAMATIC MUSIC]

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.800
The rules to me are
very, very simple.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.700
Your job is to tell a story.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:46.320
A story has a hero, and
he or she wants one thing.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.750
If it doesn't tend toward
the punchline, take it out.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.374
Gossip is just
creating dialogue.

00:00:50.374 --> 00:00:52.290
Billy says to him, "Blah,
blah, blah," and Bob

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:53.580
said, "Bibbity, bobbity, boo."

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:56.046
That's as good a dialogue
as anybody else can write.

00:00:56.046 --> 00:00:57.950
[PIANO MUSIC]

00:00:57.950 --> 00:01:00.230
I would hope that you
guys would be encouraged

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:03.620
to say, if this class
clown can do so well, hell,

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.390
I'll try it too.

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:08.810
I'm not any less
confused than you are.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.560
I just got in the
habit of doing it.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.450
We all have ideas all the time.

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.690
Our mind is a raging fire.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.500
At some point you're going to
say, OK, it's going to be bad.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.660
You've got to stand
being bad if you

00:01:23.660 --> 00:01:26.030
want to be a writer because
if you don't, you're never

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:28.930
going to write anything good.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.520
We're given a great gift
and a great responsibility,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:33.400
which is to tell stories
as honestly as we

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:40.120
can and experience things in
the process that are beautiful.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:44.100
I'm David Mamet, and
this is my Master Class.

